  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nBy Sat night tropical moisture will be entrenched across the \nsoutheastern U.S with the frontal boundary stalled just north of the \nstate. The front is expected to stay near the North GA border \nthrough Mon morning before it begins to make its way southward into \nS GA/N FL Tue. With this in mind the frontal boundary will act as a \nblock not allowing the tropical moisture to move out of the region \nwhile moisture continues to flow from the Gulf in the southerly \nflow. This will put GA in a prime location to see multiple inches of \nprecipitation Sun and Mon across the entire area then continuing \nacross central GA Tue Tue through Thu. \n\nThe models have begun to settle in on South/Central GA seeing the \nhighest precip totals through the middle of next week. Currently \nseeing precip totals in the 2 to 4.5 inch range across central GA \nand 1.5 to 3 inches across North GA through Day 7. Due to the \nlimited amount of precipitation we have seen over the past few \nweeks, central GA can handle a bit more rain than north GA but 2 to \n4.5 inches is still a lot of water in just 3 to 5 days. Will still \nneed to keep a close eye on the flooding potential across the area. \nWPC has issued an Excessive Rain Outlook for the entire area Sat and \nSun and across portions of central GA MOn.  Still not seeing much in \nthe way of instability across the area but we could still see some \nthunderstorms Fri and Sat. SPC only has us under a general risk of \nsevere which continues to look good through the weekend. \n\nBy daybreak Tue the the frontal boundary will be moving south \nushering in a much drier and cooler airmass into North GA. Temps \nwill beginning to cool as the precip moves in Sat. High temps Sun \nexpected in the 70s to middle 80s with high Mon through Thu expected \nin the 70s.",
